{
  "title": "Design: one decoded private-strings check before credential redaction and before any cut, with explicit truncation flags",
  "body": "Slice (c1) of #880. It's a design document only, with no source changes. #919 (c2) implements it.\n\n## Why design first\n#909 tried to fix #880's ordering and truncation gaps directly. It grew to 689 lines against a 40-line budget, and review still found six defects, all with one cause. The authoritative decoded private-strings check doesn't run on the complete text before credential redaction and before every cut. Examples:\n- **Gate capture:** capture matches literals while streaming. A credential that overlaps a private value erases its start, and a `.`-escaped remainder then reaches the stored event and the model prompt.\n- **Failure excerpts and the gate tail:** both clip text and drop the clipping metadata, so a private value's prefix survives.\n- **Length heuristics:** a complete 4,000-character message is treated as clipped and wrongly withheld.\n- **MCP HTTP errors:** these are sanitized before their JSON is parsed.\n\nThe obvious fix, running the full decoded check before every cut, is too slow on its own. On main, `privateMatches` already takes about 1.3 s per 5 MiB of gate output with eight entries. So the mechanism has to be designed before it's built.\n\n## Deliverable\nA new document, `docs/privacy-boundaries.md`, linked from docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §6 or the privacy section, containing:\n1. **An inventory of every boundary** where text that may hold a private string or credential leaves the process or is stored. Give each one's file:function, the order of private check, credential redaction and cuts today, and whether truncation is known there. Start from:\n   - gate output capture and the 6,000-character tail (src/gates/run.ts);\n   - failure excerpts (src/gates/failures.ts);\n   - stored gate and agent events (src/pipeline/context.ts);\n   - prompts built from gate diagnostics, and the final prompt dispatch;\n   - post-merge comparisons and their persistence (`redactPostMergeComparisons`);\n   - `WorkerStore.sanitize`;\n   - MCP results, errors and the HTTP backend (src/integrations/mcp*.ts);\n   - `privateOutputData`, the CLI digest and the feed;\n   - PR comments and commit metadata.\n   Find the others with `rg` for `redactCredentials`, `privateMatches`, `slice(`, `truncate` and the `*_TAIL` constants, and say how you searched.\n2. **One rule and one mechanism.**\n   - The rule: at every boundary, the authoritative decoded check runs on complete text before credential redaction, and before any cut.\n   - Where text is cut first, the cut carries an explicit `truncated` flag, set only when the source really exceeded its limit and never inferred from length.\n   - Name the single function or type every boundary calls, and how streams (gate output) keep this affordable. For example: a bounded window carried across chunks, sized from the longest entry and the maximum escape expansion; or one check per finished stream before the tail is taken. Estimate the cost at 5 MiB with 8 entries and 600 credentials.\n3. **The adversarial test list (c2) must pass,** one per boundary class. It includes #909's six reproductions: Unicode-overlap capture, mixed literal and escaped HTTP error JSON, excerpt provenance, a gate-tail cut inside an escape, an exact-limit message that isn't clipped, and the ReDoS input (fixed in slice (a)).\n4. **Compatibility:**\n   - stored rows written before the change stay readable;\n   - the previous release still runs after a rollback;\n   - no change to what the owner sees beyond withholding more when unsure.\n5. **c2's line budget**, and the slices c2 should be cut into if the budget exceeds about 250 lines.\n\n## Rules\n- No source or test changes in this slice. The document is the whole diff.\n- Use placeholders (`host.example`, `\u003ccredential\u003e`) for every example value. Never use real hostnames or secrets.\n\n## Out of scope\nImplementing any of it (#919); `\\/` decoding (#917); the ReDoS fix (#916); adversarial encodings (#335); credentials split across lines.\n\nBudget: one document, about 150–300 lines.\n"
}
