  "detail": "Thank you: the inventory, the provenance contract (true / false / unknown, with no length heuristics), the cut-then-recheck rule with the NFKC combining-mark example, and the five dependency-ordered c2 slices are what #919 needs.\n\nOne gap. The cost section budgets one to three complete checks of 5 MiB per gate, estimated at 1.3 to 3.9 s of CPU, but it doesn't say where that work runs. Run synchronously in the daemon, each check is an event-loop stall of that length. AGENTS.md's hot-path rule asks for the worst stall, and the daemon already logs stalls over its threshold. Add to the design:\n(1) Where complete-text checks run: for example a worker thread per check, or bounded synchronous slices with yields between them. Name the choice and why.\n(2) A stall budget, such as no single synchronous slice over 50 ms on the daemon thread for any input size.\n(3) Make the c2.2 benchmark report the worst event-loop stall beside the medians, and add it to the c2.1/c2.2 acceptance.\nAlso update the baseline: since #921, and with #923 (`\\/` decoding plus layer caching) about to land, `privateMatches` on 5 MiB with eight entries measured about 110 to 260 ms here (scripts/benchmark-credential-redaction.ts), against the 1.3 s figure quoted from the issue. Keep the 1.3 s as the issue's figure, but plan from the measured one. State that \"supported\" means whatever `normalizedLayers` does at c2's base, including #917's `\\/` decoding once it lands."
